\title{Unique Error-Correcting Reconstruction of Polynomial Secret Shares}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{August 2, 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Polynomial secret sharing encodes a secret as the constant coefficient of a low-degree polynomial and distributes evaluations of that polynomial at distinct field elements. We establish the uniqueness core of error-correcting reconstruction. Given received values at $n$ distinct locations, there is at most one polynomial of degree at most $d$ that disagrees with those values in at most $e$ positions whenever $n\ge d+2e+1$. Consequently, the encoded secret is uniquely determined under the same condition. The proof isolates the union of the disagreement sets of two candidate polynomials, leaving at least $d+1$ locations where both candidates agree; polynomial interpolation then forces equality. We relate the result to the minimum distance $n-d$ of Reed--Solomon evaluation codes, distinguish uniqueness from decoding existence, describe finite-field algorithms, and discuss applications to robust secret sharing, distributed storage, and coded computation.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
Secret sharing replaces a single vulnerable copy of a secret with distributed shares. In Shamir's polynomial construction, a dealer works over a field $F$, chooses a polynomial whose constant coefficient is the secret, and gives participants evaluations at distinct nonzero field elements. A sufficiently large collection determines the polynomial by interpolation and hence reveals the secret.

Elementary reconstruction assumes that every supplied share is correct. Real shares may be corrupted by hardware failure, transmission errors, stale state, or malicious behavior. Reconstruction therefore raises two separate questions: whether a low-degree polynomial exists within a bounded number of disagreements, and whether such a polynomial is unique. This paper addresses uniqueness. For degree at most $d$ and at most $e$ errors, $d+2e+1$ distinct received locations suffice.

The argument is direct. If two candidates each reject at most $e$ received values, then together they reject at most $2e$ locations. At every remaining location both equal the same received value. At least $d+1$ remaining locations force equality by polynomial rigidity. This also explains why an unknown error costs twice as much redundancy as an ordinary interpolation constraint: competing explanations may place their errors at disjoint locations.

The result applies wherever low-degree polynomial evaluation serves as an integrity-preserving representation, including robust secret sharing, Reed--Solomon storage, secure multiparty computation, coded distributed linear algebra, and fault-tolerant aggregation.

\section{Algebraic setting}
\subsection{Polynomials and interpolation}
Let $F$ be a field and $F[X]$ its univariate polynomial ring. For $p\in F[X]$ and $x\in F$, write $p(x)$ for evaluation at $x$. The zero polynomial is included among polynomials of degree at most $d$.

\begin{lemma}[Root bound]
If a nonzero polynomial $h\in F[X]$ has degree at most $d$, then it has at most $d$ distinct roots in $F$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
By the factor theorem, each distinct root contributes a distinct linear factor. A product of $m$ such factors has degree $m$ and divides $h$, so $m\le \deg h\le d$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Agreement determines a bounded-degree polynomial]
If $p,q\in F[X]$ have degree at most $d$ and agree at $d+1$ distinct field elements, then $p=q$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
The difference $p-q$ has degree at most $d$ and has every agreement location as a root. If it were nonzero, the root bound would permit at most $d$ such locations.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Polynomial secret sharing}
A degree-$d$ sharing instance selects
\[
p(X)=s+a_1X+\cdots+a_dX^d,
\]
where the secret is $s=p(0)$. At pairwise distinct locations $x_1,\ldots,x_n\in F$, the shares are $(x_i,p(x_i))$. Locations are part of the data. Traditional sharing uses nonzero locations so no individual receives the secret directly; the uniqueness theorem needs only distinctness.

\subsection{Received words and disagreement sets}
Let $L\subseteq F$ be a finite set and let $r:L\to F$ assign a received value to every location.

\begin{definition}[Disagreement set]
For $p\in F[X]$, define
\[
D_{L,r}(p)=\{x\in L:p(x)\ne r(x)\}.
\]
Its cardinality is the Hamming distance between the evaluation vector of $p$ and the received vector. The polynomial is \emph{$e$-consistent} when $|D_{L,r}(p)|\le e$.
\end{definition}

No existence assumption is implicit in this definition. A received vector may have no $e$-consistent candidate.

\section{The combinatorial agreement principle}
\begin{lemma}[Agreement outside disagreement sets]
If $x\in L$ lies in neither $D_{L,r}(p)$ nor $D_{L,r}(q)$, then $p(x)=q(x)$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Both values equal $r(x)$.
\end{proof}

For two candidates define
\[
B=D_{L,r}(p)\cup D_{L,r}(q),\qquad G=L\setminus B.
\]
If each candidate has at most $e$ disagreements, then
\[
|B|\le |D_{L,r}(p)|+|D_{L,r}(q)|\le 2e.
\]
Since $B\subseteq L$,
\[
|G|=|L|-|B|.
\]
Thus $|L|\ge d+2e+1$ implies $|G|\ge d+1$, and the candidates agree throughout $G$.

\section{Main results}
\begin{theorem}[Unique error-correcting reconstruction]
Let $F$ be a field, $L\subseteq F$ a finite set of distinct locations, and $r:L\to F$ a received vector. Let $d,e\ge0$ satisfy
\[
|L|\ge d+2e+1.
\]
If $p,q\in F[X]$ have degree at most $d$ and
\[
|D_{L,r}(p)|\le e,\qquad |D_{L,r}(q)|\le e,
\]
then $p=q$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Let $B$ and $G$ be as above. The union bound gives $|B|\le2e$, hence
\[
|G|=|L|-|B|\ge d+1.
\]
At every $x\in G$, neither polynomial disagrees with the received value, so
\[
p(x)=r(x)=q(x).
\]
The candidates agree at at least $d+1$ distinct points. The bounded-degree agreement lemma yields $p=q$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Uniqueness of the reconstructed secret]
Under the theorem's hypotheses, the constant coefficient of an $e$-consistent degree-at-most-$d$ candidate is unique. In particular, any two such candidates satisfy $p(0)=q(0)$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Evaluate the polynomial equality at $0$.
\end{proof}

Writing $n=|L|$, the threshold is equivalent to $2e<n-d$. Thus the largest guaranteed unique-decoding radius is
\[
e_{\max}=\left\lfloor\frac{n-d-1}{2}\right\rfloor.
\]
For $e=0$, the theorem is ordinary interpolation from $d+1$ values. For $d=0$, it is strict-majority uniqueness from $2e+1$ values.

\section{Reed--Solomon interpretation}
Order $L=\{x_1,\ldots,x_n\}$. The evaluation map is
\[
\operatorname{ev}_L(p)=(p(x_1),\ldots,p(x_n))\in F^n.
\]
Its image on degree-at-most-$d$ polynomials is a Reed--Solomon evaluation code.

\begin{proposition}[Distance lower bound]
Evaluation vectors of two distinct degree-at-most-$d$ polynomials differ in at least $n-d$ coordinates.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
The two polynomials can agree only where their nonzero difference vanishes. That difference has degree at most $d$ and hence at most $d$ roots among the locations.
\end{proof}

When the field has at least $n$ elements and $d<n$, this bound is attained, so the minimum distance is $n-d$. Alternatively, if a received vector $r$ lies within distance $e$ of codewords $c_p$ and $c_q$, the Hamming triangle inequality gives
\[
\operatorname{dist}(c_p,c_q)
\le \operatorname{dist}(c_p,r)+\operatorname{dist}(r,c_q)
\le2e.
\]
Distinct candidates are therefore impossible when $2e<n-d$. This is the geometric form of the disagreement-set proof.

\section{Constructive procedures}
\subsection{Candidate consistency testing}
Horner's rule evaluates a degree-at-most-$d$ polynomial in $O(d)$ field operations. Testing it at $n$ locations and counting disagreements costs $O(nd)$ field operations.

\begin{algorithm}[Candidate consistency test]
Given coefficients of $p$, labeled received values, and budget $e$:
\begin{enumerate}[label=\arabic*.]
\item Initialize a mismatch counter to zero.
\item At each location, evaluate $p$ by Horner's rule.
\item Increment the counter when the result differs from the received value.
\item Reject immediately if the counter exceeds $e$.
\item Otherwise accept after all locations have been processed.
\end{enumerate}
\end{algorithm}

Under the main theorem's threshold, two distinct accepted degree-at-most-$d$ candidates cannot exist.

\subsection{Exhaustive finite-field decoding}
Over a small prime field $\mathbf F_p$, enumerate all $p^{d+1}$ coefficient tuples, evaluate each polynomial, and retain candidates with at most $e$ disagreements. This costs $O(p^{d+1}nd)$ field operations. It is unsuitable for cryptographic parameters but useful for transparent examples. Under the uniqueness threshold the resulting list has size at most one.

\subsection{Toward efficient decoding}
The Berlekamp--Welch method introduces an error-locator polynomial $E$ of degree at most $e$ and $Q$ of degree at most $d+e$, constrained by
\[
Q(x_i)=r_iE(x_i)
\]
at every location. These are linear constraints on coefficients after normalization. When the received word is close to $p$, one seeks $Q=Ep$ and recovers $p$ by division. A full existence theorem lies beyond the result proved here, but uniqueness guarantees that any recovered candidate within the radius is the only one.

\section{Worked example}
Work in $\mathbf F_{17}$ with
\[
p(X)=5+3X+2X^2.
\]
The secret is $5$. At locations $1,2,3,4,5$, the evaluations are
\[
(10,2,15,15,2)\pmod {17}.
\]
Corrupt the third value to obtain
\[
r=(10,2,4,15,2).
\]
The original polynomial has one disagreement. Here $n=5$, $d=2$, and $e=1$, so $n=d+2e+1$. A second $1$-consistent quadratic would jointly reject at most two locations with the original, leaving at least three common agreements. Two quadratics agreeing at three distinct locations are equal. Thus the only possible secret within radius one is $5$.

Below the threshold ambiguity can occur. For $n=4,d=2,e=1$ over a sufficiently large field, choose distinct quadratics agreeing at two locations. At each of the other two locations, let the received vector take one value from each polynomial. Both candidates then have one disagreement.

\section{Applications}
\paragraph{Robust threshold sharing.}
Ordinary reconstruction of degree-at-most-$d$ sharing needs $d+1$ correct shares. With at most $e$ malicious submissions, $d+2e+1$ distinct labeled shares prevent two secrets from being compatible with the data inside the error budget.

\paragraph{Distributed storage.}
Reed--Solomon storage represents data by evaluations across nodes. The same threshold ensures unique codeword recovery under symbol corruption. Unknown errors cost twice as much redundancy as known erasures because their locations must also be inferred.

\paragraph{Secure multiparty computation.}
Many protocols maintain polynomially shared state. The theorem supplies the uniqueness foundation for robust opening: enough shares relative to degree and corruption budget exclude two low-degree outcomes.

\paragraph{Coded and private distributed learning.}
Polynomial codes distribute matrix operations, aggregation, and private transformations among workers. If the desired result is encoded by a known degree bound, $n\ge d+2e+1$ prevents ambiguous decoding under $e$ erroneous responses.

\section{Limitations and scope}
\subsection{Parameter selection in practice}
The threshold is a capacity budget. With $n$ available responses and degree bound $d$, the worst-case adversarial tolerance guaranteed by the theorem is
\[
\left\lfloor\frac{n-d-1}{2}\right\rfloor.
\]
Conversely, tolerating $e$ errors requires at least $d+2e+1$ responses. This count concerns responses actually available at reconstruction time, not merely enrolled participants. Missing evaluations reduce the usable value of $n$ and should be budgeted separately from erroneous responses: an absence is identifiable, whereas an erroneous response consumes additional redundancy because its location is not known in advance. The field and location-allocation policy must also provide enough pairwise distinct evaluation points. The guarantee is information-theoretic and does not require a probability distribution on failures; it remains valid for adaptively chosen erroneous values.

\subsection{Logical scope}
The theorem proves at most one candidate, not at least one, and neither locates errors nor supplies an efficient decoder. Distinct locations and a valid degree bound are essential. It concerns adversarial symbol errors under Hamming distance; probabilistic noise, incorrect labels, and soft information require other models. Privacy against sub-threshold coalitions is logically separate from reconstruction uniqueness. Beyond half the minimum distance, unique decoding may fail and list decoding becomes the appropriate objective.

\section{Future directions}
\paragraph{Sharpness.}
Under suitable field-size and parameter assumptions, construct two distinct degree-at-most-$d$ polynomials and one received vector within $e$ disagreements of both whenever $n\le d+2e$.

\paragraph{Berlekamp--Welch existence.}
Prove that when a received vector at $n\ge d+2e+1$ locations differs from some degree-at-most-$d$ polynomial in at most $e$ positions, the error-locator system has a nontrivial solution and division recovers that polynomial.

\paragraph{Errors and erasures.}
With $s$ known erasures and $e$ unknown errors, establish uniqueness under
\[
n\ge d+s+2e+1
\]
and prove sharpness under appropriate feasibility conditions.

\paragraph{Beyond half distance.}
Construct explicit received vectors with multiple nearby candidates when $2e>n-d-1$, marking the transition from unique to list decoding.

\section{Conclusion}
Two candidates within $e$ errors of one received vector can collectively question no more than $2e$ locations. From at least $d+2e+1$ supplied locations, at least $d+1$ uncontested locations remain. Agreement there and the polynomial root bound force equality. Thus both the polynomial and its constant coefficient are unique. This compact argument is simultaneously the foundation of robust polynomial secret reconstruction and the half-minimum-distance guarantee for Reed--Solomon codes.

